---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/alternatives/privy-alternative";
const title = "Privy alternatives: replace displays without breaking the welcome promise";
const desc = "Decide whether to keep Privy, replace only capture, or move messaging too. Preserve live offers, channel preferences and follow-up obligations before retiring a display.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Privy only a popup tool?",
    "answer": "No. Its current website markets popups, email, SMS and journey automation. First identify what the actual account owns before adding another sender or choosing a capture-only replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does the legacy Shopify listing mean Privy closed?",
    "answer": "No. At this review the legacy listing says it is not currently available and directs support questions to Privy Operations. Confirm the supported account connection and recovery path with Privy before uninstalling a working installation."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should old signups restart a welcome series?",
    "answer": "Not by default. Preserve which offer was issued, what was delivered and what remains due. Test re-entry rules, purchases and withdrawals; moving a contact record should not silently issue another incentive or replay completed messages."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<main class="container py-14 md:py-20">
<a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
<p class="badge mt-6">Replacement planning · November 25, 2026</p>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
<p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Retain the incumbent when the complete journey fits</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy's current offering</a> includes targeted popups, email, SMS and visual journeys. That combination can be useful when one team maintains the signup promise and its follow-up in one place. Compare the actual enabled workflow before assuming Privy only captures leads. Repair a faulty display rule or message handoff before paying to rebuild the entire journey.</p>
<p>Checked November 25, 2026, the <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/privy">legacy Shopify listing</a> says it is not currently available and names Privy Operations for support. This does not establish closure or loss of existing service. The <a href="/apps/privy">Privy app review</a> covers account and connection discovery. This replacement plan begins after that inventory: obtain the supported recovery path before removing an app, script or authorization that a working journey depends on.</p>
<h2>Inventory each display as a promise and a writer</h2>
<p>Make a row for every popup, embedded form, landing page, quiz and mobile variant actually installed. Do not assume a quiz exists just because a product offers one. Save creative and targeting rules along with the success screen, linked destination and follow-up. Record which theme, page or external placement loads the display so retiring one editor entry does not leave another signup route active.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Display record</th><th>What must survive</th><th>Replacement exercise</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Offer and success state</td><td>Value, eligibility, expiry and delivery channel</td><td>New visitor receives the promised benefit once</td></tr>
<tr><td>Returning visitor rules</td><td>Prior signup, dismissal and customer state</td><td>Existing buyer is not treated as a new subscriber</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preference or quiz field</td><td>Question version, answer values and profile destination</td><td>Changed answer updates the intended field without overwriting unrelated data</td></tr>
<tr><td>Permission control</td><td>Independent email/SMS choices and disclosure version</td><td>Email yes and SMS no remain distinct downstream</td></tr>
<tr><td>Integration action</td><td>Destination account, identifier, tags and trigger</td><td>One submission creates the intended next action exactly once</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Audit mobile close controls and the fallback when a script fails. Compare the displayed promise with the first email or text: an access-only signup should not become a blanket discount audience. Preserve source and timestamp evidence where available, and label missing evidence. A raw phone field is not SMS permission.</p>
<h2>Preserve preference meaning, not just contact totals</h2>
<p>Privy's <a href="https://www.privy.com/changelog">October 15 changelog</a> describes an email Preference Center: a published page lets subscribers choose topics, and campaigns with a selected topic skip contacts who opted out of it. An export of active email addresses alone may therefore miss a meaningful restriction. Identify whether the account uses this feature and which topics each campaign selects.</p>
<p>Build a mapping for global channel withdrawal, topic choices, product interests and source tags. They are different concepts. A request to stop promotional email must not be lost because a contact is still interested in one product. If a destination cannot express a topic rule, define a conservative exclusion and a customer-facing preference path before activation rather than silently broadening permission.</p>
<p>Use proposed test contacts for email-only signup, SMS-only signup where supported, returning customer, topic opt-out, global withdrawal and a changed answer. Inspect the final send audience as well as the destination profile. Reconcile unmatched identities and stale writes explicitly; a later import should not overwrite a newer withdrawal. See <a href="/guides/consent">permission evidence</a> and <a href="/guides/list-growth">capture planning</a>.</p>
<h2>Close issued offers before rebuilding welcome flows</h2>
<p>A display may issue a code on its success screen while a welcome message reminds the shopper later. Record where each code is created, its validity and restrictions, whether it has already been delivered and whether it has been redeemed. Replacing the display should not invalidate an outstanding promise without an agreed customer-service plan. Rebuilding the welcome should not grant a second new-customer incentive to imported contacts.</p>
<p>For an illustrative gift signup, keep gift-delivery guidance separate from self-purchase product education. For a limited pottery release, keep the waitlist interest and access priority without promising stock to everyone. For a subscription customer, demonstrate exclusion from an incompatible acquisition offer. These preserve useful calendar and targeting questions from the old page without presenting invented merchant outcomes.</p>
<p>Test a purchase after signup but before the welcome reminder, an expired code and a customer who submits twice on different devices. Record what remains due, including an undelivered benefit or support reply. Cancel or reconstruct the remaining step deliberately; importing a list is not proof that a journey resumes at the correct point.</p>
<h2>Choose candidates against the boundary being replaced</h2>
<p>For capture-only work, ask <a href="/apps/justuno">Justuno</a> to reproduce the display rule, preference field and destination handoff using the same fixture. For a combined journey, ask <a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> or <a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> to show display-to-message behavior and permission changes. Those are demonstration requests, not a ranking or a claim that old rules transfer automatically.</p>
<p>If the unresolved job is SMS release execution, ask <a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> or <a href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> to demonstrate audience updates, replies and purchase exits. That does not replace the capture and email work by itself. If considering <a href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> for email, verify the required fields, events and suppression path rather than assuming a Privy integration or a cheaper combined stack. Keep a written owner for every remaining function.</p>
<h2>Test one display owner without losing new submissions</h2>
<ol><li>Save a versioned inventory and a checkpoint of contact status, issued offers and queued follow-up.</li><li>Prepare the replacement on a controlled test page or exclusive traffic slice. Avoid two competing popups or two welcome triggers serving the same visitor.</li><li>Submit the fixture cases on desktop and mobile. Inspect the success state, stored fields, permitted channel and actual next message.</li><li>Disable the outgoing display path and verify it no longer accepts production submissions. Capture changes since the checkpoint, including new opt-outs and purchases, before activating the replacement audience.</li><li>Verify scheduled and queued jobs separately from the display switch. Assign one pause owner and a route for delayed or failed submissions.</li><li>For rollback, restore the accepted display version only after reconciling newly issued codes, completed messages and preference changes. Do not replay the old welcome to everyone.</li></ol>
<p>Do not uninstall the outgoing connection until the supported recovery and retention paths are understood. A side-by-side design preview is useful; an uncontrolled parallel live send is not an acceptance method. Check recipient-time rules and current destination requirements for the actual audience with the provider. Use <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> to record ownership.</p>
<h2>Measure accepted signups and total economics</h2>
<p>Compare eligible visitors, display exposures, submissions, accepted channel permissions, successful handoffs and first-message delivery using consistent denominators. A higher popup submission rate can conceal duplicate contacts or missing downstream permission. Inspect dismissals and support questions as well as attributed orders. Where feasible, use an exclusive traffic allocation and a suitable holdout; report the observation window and limitations rather than inventing uplift.</p>
<p>Illustrative offer math: $80 merchandise value less an $8 incentive yields $72 collected; less $32 goods cost leaves $40 before fulfillment, messaging, returns and support. If net sales already reflect the discount, do not subtract it again. Quote contact and send tiers, SMS units and carrier charges, display scope, services and overlap labor at current and projected volume. Use <a href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy</a> and candidate proposals for current terms, plus the <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a>; no historic plan price is carried forward.</p>
<p>Approve replacement only when the new display fulfills the same accepted promise, preferences reach the audience and one owner can pause every follow-up. Keep unresolved exceptions with an owner. These are proposed checks, not vendor-account tests or a promise of improvement. Continue with <a href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a>, <a href="/use-cases/welcome-series">welcome planning</a> and <a href="/guides/campaign-restraint">calendar restraint</a> for the jobs that remain.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</main>
</BaseLayout>
